1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:08,554
Tokyo, January 26, 1948.

2
00:00:08,574 --> 00:00:11,244
Just after three in the afternoon,

3
00:00:11,494 --> 00:00:16,804
the Shiinamachi branch of the Imperial Bank has closed its doors for the day.

4
00:00:16,845 --> 00:00:20,515
Inside, sixteen people are finishing their work.

5
00:00:20,593 --> 00:00:24,503
Within half an hour, twelve of them will be dead,

6
00:00:24,673 --> 00:00:29,383
and the man responsible will walk out the front door with the bank's cash.

7
00:00:30,069 --> 00:00:33,969
This is the Teigin Incident, one of the strangest

8
00:00:33,989 --> 00:00:37,759
and most disputed crimes in Japanese history.

9
00:00:47,329 --> 00:00:52,179
To understand how it happened, you have to picture Tokyo at the time.

10
00:00:52,272 --> 00:00:55,862
The war had ended less than three years earlier.

11
00:00:55,934 --> 00:01:00,064
The city was still scarred by bombing, food was scarce,

12
00:01:00,374 --> 00:01:03,224
and the country was under American occupation.

13
00:01:03,522 --> 00:01:06,902
Outbreaks of infectious disease were common,

14
00:01:06,922 --> 00:01:09,202
and health officials carrying orders from the

15
00:01:09,222 --> 00:01:13,032
occupation authorities were a familiar sight.

16
00:01:13,052 --> 00:01:16,322
People were used to following them without question.

17
00:01:17,130 --> 00:01:22,152
General MacArthur's headquarters, known to everyone simply as GHQ,

18
00:01:22,172 --> 00:01:27,920
issued directives that reached into almost every corner of daily life.

19
00:01:27,940 --> 00:01:30,490
Public health was one of its priorities.

20
00:01:30,984 --> 00:01:34,224
Crowded housing, damaged water systems and

21
00:01:34,244 --> 00:01:37,344
poor nutrition made epidemics a constant fear,

22
00:01:37,364 --> 00:01:42,684
and teams of Japanese health workers moved from district to district,

23
00:01:42,704 --> 00:01:48,114
spraying disinfectant, giving inoculations and handing out instructions.

24
00:01:48,513 --> 00:01:52,198
A man with an official armband who said that GHQ

25
00:01:52,218 --> 00:01:56,651
required something was not a man you argued with.

26
00:01:56,901 --> 00:02:00,221
That habit of trust, sensible in its time,

27
00:02:00,241 --> 00:02:03,151
is the key to everything that happened next.

28
00:02:08,153 --> 00:02:14,398
Around 3:20 pm, a middle-aged man knocked at the bank's side entrance.

29
00:02:14,418 --> 00:02:18,898
He wore an armband that marked him as a public health official

30
00:02:18,918 --> 00:02:23,922
and handed over a business card identifying him as a doctor.

31
00:02:24,172 --> 00:02:28,482
He explained that there had been an outbreak of dysentery nearby.

32
00:02:28,688 --> 00:02:32,468
The American occupation authorities, he said,

33
00:02:32,488 --> 00:02:35,188
had ordered that everyone at the bank take a preventive

34
00:02:35,208 --> 00:02:38,878
medicine before anyone was allowed to leave.

35
00:02:39,028 --> 00:02:41,778
He was calm, polite, and confident.

36
00:02:41,986 --> 00:02:44,796
Nobody had reason to doubt him.

37
00:02:49,612 --> 00:02:52,752
The man gathered the staff, along with members

38
00:02:52,772 --> 00:02:56,222
of the caretaker's family, including children.

39
00:02:56,389 --> 00:02:58,379
Sixteen people in all.

40
00:02:58,644 --> 00:03:03,234
He set out cups and showed them exactly how to take the medicine:

41
00:03:03,404 --> 00:03:07,734
two doses, one after the other, about a minute apart.

42
00:03:07,853 --> 00:03:11,083
He even drank first to show them it was safe.

43
00:03:11,953 --> 00:03:15,563
Survivors later recalled how practised he seemed.

44
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:19,140
He measured the liquid into each cup with a dropper,

45
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:21,200
and explained that the first dose was

46
00:03:21,220 --> 00:03:24,300
strong and should be swallowed in one go,

47
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:29,170
pushed to the back of the tongue, so that it would not harm their teeth.

48
00:03:29,849 --> 00:03:31,779
They did as he said.

49
00:03:31,891 --> 00:03:34,981
Within minutes, people began collapsing.

50
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:41,210
Some managed to stagger outside for water or help before falling unconscious.

51
00:03:41,541 --> 00:03:44,271
The liquid was a cyanide compound.

52
00:03:45,129 --> 00:03:47,419
Ten people died at the bank.

53
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:50,170
Two more died later in hospital.

54
00:03:50,705 --> 00:03:54,015
Of the sixteen, only four survived,

55
00:03:54,305 --> 00:03:57,935
and their accounts became the core of the investigation.

56
00:03:59,003 --> 00:04:02,648
When neighbours and passers-by first reached the building,

57
00:04:02,668 --> 00:04:05,974
they did not understand what they were seeing.

58
00:04:05,994 --> 00:04:10,084
Some early reports spoke of gas or food poisoning.

59
00:04:10,275 --> 00:04:13,155
People went inside to help, and by the time

60
00:04:13,175 --> 00:04:16,925
detectives arrived, the scene had been disturbed.

61
00:04:17,703 --> 00:04:22,003
Within hours, the Metropolitan Police had begun one

62
00:04:22,023 --> 00:04:25,813
of the largest investigations in their history.

63
00:04:25,867 --> 00:04:28,955
Laboratory tests confirmed cyanide.

64
00:04:28,975 --> 00:04:32,485
The next morning the story filled the front pages,

65
00:04:32,795 --> 00:04:35,295
and a name began to appear that would follow

66
00:04:35,315 --> 00:04:39,345
the case for decades: the Teigin Incident.

67
00:04:40,072 --> 00:04:43,192
The question facing investigators was simple

68
00:04:43,212 --> 00:04:47,082
to state and almost impossible to answer.

69
00:04:47,212 --> 00:04:49,012
Who was the doctor?

70
00:04:54,112 --> 00:05:00,438
While his victims lay dying, the man collected the cash within reach:

71
00:05:00,458 --> 00:05:05,733
about 164,000 yen and a cheque for around 17,000 yen.

72
00:05:05,753 --> 00:05:11,001
Then he left. The cheque was cashed at another bank the following day.

73
00:05:11,532 --> 00:05:17,002
It was a large sum, but investigators were struck by what he did not take.

74
00:05:17,115 --> 00:05:19,425
Far more money was in the bank.

75
00:05:19,625 --> 00:05:24,463
Why go to such lengths, and kill twelve people, for only part of it?

76
00:05:24,483 --> 00:05:27,413
The question has never been fully answered.

77
00:05:32,428 --> 00:05:35,608
The four survivors had each seen the man at close range,

78
00:05:35,628 --> 00:05:38,951
in daylight, for several minutes.

79
00:05:38,971 --> 00:05:42,461
They described someone composed and authoritative,

80
00:05:42,571 --> 00:05:45,291
perhaps in his forties or fifties,

81
00:05:45,311 --> 00:05:49,281
who spoke politely and handled his bottles with ease.

82
00:05:50,004 --> 00:05:54,754
From their accounts, police produced a composite image of the suspect.

83
00:05:54,989 --> 00:05:59,254
It was printed in newspapers and posted across the country,

84
00:05:59,274 --> 00:06:04,541
and it became one of the most recognisable faces of post-war Japan.

85
00:06:04,561 --> 00:06:06,771
Thousands of tips poured in.

86
00:06:07,424 --> 00:06:09,854
But memory under shock is fragile.

87
00:06:10,297 --> 00:06:12,717
The survivors had just watched colleagues

88
00:06:12,737 --> 00:06:15,697
and family members collapse around them,

89
00:06:15,717 --> 00:06:18,907
and their recollections did not always agree.

90
00:06:19,059 --> 00:06:24,339
Over the months that followed, as they were shown photograph after photograph,

91
00:06:24,359 --> 00:06:27,749
their certainty would be tested again and again.

92
00:06:28,679 --> 00:06:33,009
Police soon discovered that Teigin was not the first attempt.

93
00:06:33,174 --> 00:06:37,394
In October 1947, a man had tried the same

94
00:06:37,414 --> 00:06:40,684
story at a branch of the Yasuda Bank,

95
00:06:40,854 --> 00:06:46,234
and on January 19, 1948, just a week before,

96
00:06:46,254 --> 00:06:48,984
at a branch of the Mitsubishi Bank.

97
00:06:49,060 --> 00:06:54,170
In both cases the staff drank harmless liquid and nobody was hurt.

98
00:06:54,718 --> 00:06:58,348
At those banks, the visitor had left business cards.

99
00:06:58,530 --> 00:07:03,160
One of them bore the name of a real doctor, Shigeru Matsui.

100
00:07:03,259 --> 00:07:06,109
Matsui had nothing to do with the crime,

101
00:07:06,299 --> 00:07:11,083
but he kept careful records of everyone he had exchanged cards with.

102
00:07:11,103 --> 00:07:13,853
Detectives began working through the list.

103
00:07:14,856 --> 00:07:18,766
In Japan, business cards were exchanged constantly,

104
00:07:18,956 --> 00:07:21,486
and they carried real social weight.

105
00:07:21,527 --> 00:07:26,279
Matsui's record turned an impossible search into a finite one.

106
00:07:26,299 --> 00:07:29,909
If the killer had used a genuine Matsui card,

107
00:07:30,019 --> 00:07:32,909
he must have received it from Matsui himself.

108
00:07:33,268 --> 00:07:36,048
So detectives set out to find every person

109
00:07:36,068 --> 00:07:39,298
on the list and ask a single question:

110
00:07:39,588 --> 00:07:42,018
can you show us the card you were given?

111
00:07:42,637 --> 00:07:47,878
Most could. Others had lost theirs but were cleared in other ways.

112
00:07:48,043 --> 00:07:50,843
The recipients were scattered across the country,

113
00:07:50,863 --> 00:07:54,383
and through the spring and summer of 1948 a

114
00:07:54,403 --> 00:07:57,783
small team of detectives travelled by slow,

115
00:07:57,803 --> 00:08:02,333
crowded trains, crossing names off one at a time.

116
00:08:02,767 --> 00:08:05,307
They believed that patient police work,

117
00:08:05,327 --> 00:08:10,357
not theories about secret wartime programmes, would solve the case.

118
00:08:10,504 --> 00:08:13,654
Their search led them north, to Hokkaido.

119
00:08:18,791 --> 00:08:23,231
Sadamichi Hirasawa was born in 1892 and

120
00:08:23,251 --> 00:08:26,594
grew up partly in the port city of Otaru.

121
00:08:26,614 --> 00:08:29,454
He became a painter, and a respected one,

122
00:08:29,474 --> 00:08:32,394
known especially for his work in tempera,

123
00:08:32,414 --> 00:08:35,564
a technique using pigment bound with egg.

124
00:08:35,738 --> 00:08:38,258
His quiet landscapes and flower studies

125
00:08:38,278 --> 00:08:41,428
were shown at major official exhibitions,

126
00:08:41,738 --> 00:08:47,708
and by the 1940s his name appeared regularly in catalogues and newspapers.

127
00:08:48,286 --> 00:08:50,876
His private life was more troubled.

128
00:08:51,202 --> 00:08:56,352
In 1925 he received a course of rabies vaccinations,

129
00:08:56,882 --> 00:09:00,992
and his family later said his personality changed afterwards.

130
00:09:01,328 --> 00:09:05,458
He had money problems, and a record of minor fraud.

131
00:09:06,004 --> 00:09:13,274
In 1947, he had met Doctor Matsui on a ferry, and the two men exchanged cards.

132
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:18,900
When detectives asked to see that card, Hirasawa could not produce it.

133
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:21,390
He said his wallet had been stolen.

134
00:09:22,459 --> 00:09:26,083
One name on that list was Sadamichi Hirasawa,

135
00:09:26,103 --> 00:09:31,200
a 56-year-old painter who was well known in the art world.

136
00:09:31,220 --> 00:09:37,890
In August 1948, police arrested him at his home in Otaru, Hokkaido.

137
00:09:38,414 --> 00:09:41,504
The case against him looked strong on paper.

138
00:09:41,692 --> 00:09:44,362
He could not produce Matsui's card.

139
00:09:44,587 --> 00:09:48,397
He had come into a sum of money he could not explain.

140
00:09:48,676 --> 00:09:51,046
And he had a past record of fraud.

141
00:09:51,486 --> 00:09:54,986
But survivors could not firmly identify him,

142
00:09:55,006 --> 00:09:58,896
and no direct physical evidence connected him to the poison.

143
00:10:04,018 --> 00:10:08,828
What happened next has to be understood in the context of the time.

144
00:10:08,918 --> 00:10:13,728
Japan's new Constitution, in force since May 1947,

145
00:10:13,938 --> 00:10:18,208
said that forced confessions could not be used as evidence,

146
00:10:18,258 --> 00:10:22,128
and that no one could be convicted on a confession alone.

147
00:10:22,245 --> 00:10:28,695
But a new Code of Criminal Procedure did not take effect until January 1949,

148
00:10:29,305 --> 00:10:33,585
and Hirasawa's case was handled under the older system,

149
00:10:33,605 --> 00:10:38,075
in which a confession was treated as the strongest evidence of all.

150
00:10:38,611 --> 00:10:42,491
Suspects could be held for long periods in police cells,

151
00:10:42,511 --> 00:10:45,001
questioned for hours at a stretch,

152
00:10:45,111 --> 00:10:49,801
with little access to a lawyer and no recording of what was said.

153
00:10:50,727 --> 00:10:54,077
Hirasawa was interrogated for weeks.

154
00:10:54,283 --> 00:10:56,593
At first he denied everything.

155
00:10:56,964 --> 00:11:00,734
Then, after more than a month in custody, he confessed.

156
00:11:00,990 --> 00:11:03,810
Soon afterwards, he took it back,

157
00:11:03,830 --> 00:11:08,240
saying the confession had been forced out of him under intense pressure.

158
00:11:08,759 --> 00:11:11,909
His supporters also pointed to his health.

159
00:11:12,038 --> 00:11:16,458
Years earlier, a rabies vaccination had reportedly left him with a

160
00:11:16,478 --> 00:11:21,988
neurological condition that could cause confusion and false memories.

161
00:11:22,186 --> 00:11:26,866
They argued that a man in that state, under relentless questioning,

162
00:11:26,886 --> 00:11:29,716
could be led to confess to almost anything.

163
00:11:30,846 --> 00:11:33,936
At his trial in the Tokyo District Court,

164
00:11:33,986 --> 00:11:37,016
Hirasawa denied the crime completely.

165
00:11:37,303 --> 00:11:41,825
His lawyers argued that the confession was coerced and unreliable,

166
00:11:41,845 --> 00:11:44,716
that the identification evidence was weak,

167
00:11:44,736 --> 00:11:48,157
and that the prosecution had never explained how a

168
00:11:48,177 --> 00:11:51,530
painter could have obtained a fast-acting poison,

169
00:11:51,550 --> 00:11:55,297
or learned to handle it with such confidence.

170
00:11:55,547 --> 00:11:58,357
The prosecution relied on the confession,

171
00:11:58,447 --> 00:12:02,597
the missing card, the unexplained money and his past.

172
00:12:02,815 --> 00:12:06,035
The gap at the centre of the case, the poison,

173
00:12:06,055 --> 00:12:09,485
pointed in a direction the court did not follow.

174
00:12:14,451 --> 00:12:16,851
Many people believed the police had the wrong

175
00:12:16,871 --> 00:12:20,821
man from the start, and there was a reason why.

176
00:12:21,346 --> 00:12:24,216
The killer had handled the poison with skill.

177
00:12:24,347 --> 00:12:28,117
He measured doses precisely, used a two-stage method,

178
00:12:28,137 --> 00:12:32,470
and knew how to make the poison act with a short delay.

179
00:12:32,490 --> 00:12:35,810
Early in the investigation, detectives suspected

180
00:12:35,830 --> 00:12:39,040
someone with military training in poisons.

181
00:12:39,438 --> 00:12:42,820
They questioned former members of the Imperial

182
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:46,592
Army's Noborito research institute and of Unit 731,

183
00:12:46,612 --> 00:12:50,660
the secret unit that had carried out biological-warfare

184
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:55,768
research and lethal experiments on prisoners during the war.

185
00:12:56,018 --> 00:12:58,768
That line of inquiry was then dropped.

186
00:12:58,955 --> 00:13:04,355
At the time, the United States had quietly granted Unit 731's leaders

187
00:13:04,375 --> 00:13:09,766
immunity from prosecution in exchange for their research data.

188
00:13:09,786 --> 00:13:12,566
Many researchers believe the occupation authorities

189
00:13:12,586 --> 00:13:17,636
did not want the investigation to expose those men.

190
00:13:17,672 --> 00:13:20,512
This has never been proven, but it remains

191
00:13:20,532 --> 00:13:23,742
the most persistent theory about the case.

192
00:13:24,796 --> 00:13:30,516
Unit 731 was based near Harbin, in occupied north-east China,

193
00:13:30,536 --> 00:13:33,386
under the command of Shirō Ishii.

194
00:13:33,408 --> 00:13:36,018
Its prisoners, most of them Chinese,

195
00:13:36,188 --> 00:13:38,608
were used in experiments that are remembered

196
00:13:38,628 --> 00:13:41,678
among the gravest crimes of the war.

197
00:13:42,223 --> 00:13:47,572
After the surrender, American officials questioned Ishii and his colleagues.

198
00:13:47,592 --> 00:13:51,112
The outcome was that the unit's leaders were never put on trial,

199
00:13:51,132 --> 00:13:54,822
and their research passed to the United States.

200
00:13:54,945 --> 00:13:58,615
For decades the arrangement stayed largely hidden.

201
00:13:58,715 --> 00:14:01,035
It came to public attention through journalists

202
00:14:01,055 --> 00:14:05,895
and historians in the 1970s and 1980s,

203
00:14:05,915 --> 00:14:08,985
long after Hirasawa had been convicted.

204
00:14:09,577 --> 00:14:13,997
Notes kept by one of the Teigin detectives, published years later,

205
00:14:14,017 --> 00:14:19,107
show that former members of these units were interviewed in 1948,

206
00:14:19,277 --> 00:14:23,047
and that some described methods resembling the crime.

207
00:14:23,245 --> 00:14:28,735
No document has been found showing that GHQ ordered the inquiry stopped.

208
00:14:28,828 --> 00:14:31,178
The connection remains a theory.

209
00:14:36,304 --> 00:14:40,474
In 1950, Hirasawa was sentenced to death.

210
00:14:40,628 --> 00:14:45,658
Japan's Supreme Court upheld the sentence in 1955.

211
00:14:46,307 --> 00:14:49,097
But the sentence was never carried out.

212
00:14:49,223 --> 00:14:54,053
In Japan, an execution needs the justice minister's signature,

213
00:14:54,343 --> 00:14:57,473
and minister after minister refused to sign.

214
00:14:57,728 --> 00:15:02,818
Hirasawa's lawyers filed request after request for a retrial.

215
00:15:02,863 --> 00:15:04,853
All of them were rejected.

216
00:15:05,955 --> 00:15:09,055
Governments rose and fell, and with each new

217
00:15:09,075 --> 00:15:13,045
cabinet a new justice minister was handed the file.

218
00:15:13,074 --> 00:15:14,874
None of them signed.

219
00:15:14,944 --> 00:15:20,274
They rarely explained why in public, but the hesitation spoke for itself.

220
00:15:20,506 --> 00:15:24,076
The conviction rested on a retracted confession,

221
00:15:24,186 --> 00:15:29,316
and doubts about the poison and the identification had never gone away.

222
00:15:29,896 --> 00:15:32,266
The result was a strange limbo.

223
00:15:32,471 --> 00:15:36,241
Hirasawa was neither freed nor executed.

224
00:15:36,496 --> 00:15:39,196
In an era when condemned prisoners were usually

225
00:15:39,216 --> 00:15:42,706
given little or no warning of their execution,

226
00:15:43,036 --> 00:15:47,546
he lived with that uncertainty not for months, but for decades.

227
00:15:48,826 --> 00:15:52,436
Outside the prison, the case never went quiet.

228
00:15:52,595 --> 00:15:56,355
In the early 1960s, the writer Tetsurō Morikawa

229
00:15:56,375 --> 00:16:00,885
founded an association to win Hirasawa's release.

230
00:16:01,037 --> 00:16:04,826
The crime novelist Seichō Matsumoto wrote about

231
00:16:04,846 --> 00:16:08,311
the case and the possibility of a cover-up,

232
00:16:08,331 --> 00:16:13,917
and in 1964 the director Kei Kumai made a feature film about it.

233
00:16:13,937 --> 00:16:20,581
In English, William Triplett published a book-length investigation in 1985.

234
00:16:20,831 --> 00:16:24,661
Through it all, Hirasawa kept painting in his cell,

235
00:16:24,731 --> 00:16:27,581
and his supporters exhibited the work outside.

236
00:16:27,971 --> 00:16:30,051
To them, it was proof that the man was still

237
00:16:30,071 --> 00:16:33,361
present, still working, still waiting.

238
00:16:38,413 --> 00:16:43,443
Sadamichi Hirasawa spent almost 39 years in prison,

239
00:16:43,753 --> 00:16:46,683
more than 32 of them on death row.

240
00:16:46,769 --> 00:16:48,619
He kept painting throughout.

241
00:16:48,981 --> 00:16:57,151
On May 10, 1987, he died of pneumonia in a prison hospital at the age of 95,

242
00:16:57,501 --> 00:17:00,011
still insisting he was innocent.

243
00:17:01,097 --> 00:17:04,087
The Teigin Incident left a long shadow.

244
00:17:04,290 --> 00:17:08,430
It was one of the deadliest robberies in Japanese history,

245
00:17:08,450 --> 00:17:11,990
carried out not with weapons but with an armband,

246
00:17:12,010 --> 00:17:16,100
a story and the trust of ordinary people.

247
00:17:16,582 --> 00:17:20,392
It also became a case about justice itself.

248
00:17:20,522 --> 00:17:24,490
In the 1980s, several Japanese death-row prisoners

249
00:17:24,510 --> 00:17:28,717
were exonerated at retrial after decades behind bars,

250
00:17:28,737 --> 00:17:34,960
their convictions found to rest on confessions that could not be trusted.

251
00:17:34,980 --> 00:17:39,830
Each of those cases revived the questions about Hirasawa,

252
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:43,420
and the debate over long interrogations and the

253
00:17:43,440 --> 00:17:47,810
weight given to confessions continues in Japan today.

254
00:17:48,381 --> 00:17:53,561
And at the centre of it all are the twelve people who died in Shiinamachi:

255
00:17:53,581 --> 00:17:56,411
clerks and a caretaker's family,

256
00:17:56,481 --> 00:17:59,301
at the end of an ordinary working day,

257
00:17:59,321 --> 00:18:04,131
who did what they were told by a man they had every reason to believe.

258
00:18:05,190 --> 00:18:08,810
His adopted son continued the fight to clear his name,

259
00:18:08,830 --> 00:18:15,140
and supporters have kept pressing for a retrial even after both men's deaths.

260
00:18:15,558 --> 00:18:17,568
Officially, the case is closed.

261
00:18:17,977 --> 00:18:22,656
But the questions remain. Who was the man at the bank door,

262
00:18:22,886 --> 00:18:25,836
where did he learn to handle poison like that,

263
00:18:25,946 --> 00:18:28,666
and why did the investigation turn away from

264
00:18:28,686 --> 00:18:31,696
the people best equipped to commit the crime?
